course: dictionary
lesson: 481
title: Article: a piece of writing, a thing, or a little word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An article is a piece of writing in a newspaper, magazine or website. «The first paragraph of a news article usually tells who, what, when and where.» So when you read an article, look for the answers to those four questions near the start.
explain: An article can also be one single thing, especially one of a set. «An article of clothing» is one item, such as a scarf or a hat. Use the context to decide which meaning is right: is the word about reading or about a thing you could wear?
explain: In grammar, the articles are the small words a, an and the. Use a or an for any one thing, and the for one particular thing that both people know about. Use an before a vowel sound, as in «an article».
fact: An article is a piece of writing in a newspaper, magazine or website.
fact: A newspaper article has a headline in big or capital letters to grab you and sum up the story.
fact: In English, the articles are a, an and the.
fact: A and an mean any one thing; the means one particular thing.
fact: Some languages, like Ukrainian, have no articles: «Кіт спить» can mean «a cat sleeps» or «the cat sleeps».
exercise: «A newspaper article has a headline in big or capital letters.» What is a headline? | The last paragraph of the story | *The title in big letters that sums up the story | A picture printed in the paper | The name of the newspaper
exercise: Which words are the articles in English grammar? | *a, an and the | big, small and red | I, you and we | and, but and or
exercise: Which of these is an article of clothing? | A bus ticket | A football match | *A woolly hat | A bag of sweets
test: She read ___ article about bees in the paper. | *an | a | the | some
test: What does article mean in «a single article of clothing»? | *A single thing or object | A small word in grammar | A piece of writing | A rule in a law list
exam: A friend shares a story with «LOCAL GIRL SAVES DUCKS» in capitals and lots of likes; how can you check it is true? | Ask whether the picture looks nice | Count the likes and trust the most popular one | Trust it because it has been shared many times | *Find the real article on the outlet's own website
